  "result": "I added `split(cents: int, parts: int) -> list[int]` to `shop/money.py`, and the full test suite passes (11 tests).\n\n- **How it works:** each part gets `cents // parts`, and the leftover cents are added one at a time starting from the first element, so `split(10, 3) == [4, 3, 3]`. If `parts` is below 1 it raises `ValueError(\"parts must be at least 1: \u2026\")`.\n- **Tests:** there was no test file for `money.py`, so I created `tests/test_money.py` in the same unittest style as `tests/test_pricing.py`. It covers an even split, the remainder going to the first elements, the total always equalling `cents`, `parts=1`, and `ValueError` for `parts=0` and `parts=-1`.\n- **How I ran them:** `python3 -m unittest discover`, because `python` and pytest aren't installed here.\n- **Negative amounts:** these get no special handling. The parts still add up to `cents`, but the extra cents are rounded the other way, so `split(-10, 3) == [-3, -3, -4]` rather than `[-4, -3, -3]`. Tell me if refunds or other negative amounts should be split from the front as well.\n\nNothing is committed.",
